      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between stamped and decorative concrete?",
            "answer": "<p>Stamping presses a texture into fresh concrete to imitate stone, brick or wood. Decorative work is broader — color, acid stains, saw-cut patterns, borders and mixed finishes. Plenty of our jobs combine both. See our <a href=\"/pages/stamped-concrete-patios\">stamped patios</a> page for pattern options.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does the color last?",
            "answer": "<p>Integral color goes right through the mix, so it can't wear off the way a surface coating does. Stains and release tones sit nearer the surface — sealing every few years keeps everything rich and protects against UV fade.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you match a decorative patio to my house?",
            "answer": "<p>That's usually the whole point. We bring samples and pick colors against your brick, stone or siding on site, in daylight, before we commit to anything.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is decorative concrete slippery?",
            "answer": "<p>It doesn't have to be. Sealed smooth surfaces can get slick when wet, so we use a slip-resistant additive in the sealer on walking surfaces, steps and anywhere near water.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
